(why "" costs so much) Almost none of this work is about the empty file. The port compiles Lean's own parser and environment code from Lean source, then runs it. Before it can parse anything, even `""`, it has to build Lean's parser tables and an empty environment. That means compiling code from a 371-file import closure, and the 110 rows are the constants native Lean actually runs on that path. Each fix so far has been a general compiler feature that this code happens to use:
- expected-type propagation;
- declaration parameters in scope;
- local instances;
- types for operator operands.

(where the remaining cost is) The rows still blocked come down to a handful of causes, so a fix usually clears many rows:
- 34 rows wait on one cause, the operator types that codex-4 is fixing;
- 12 more should clear when re-probed after the `m` fix that has already landed.
